# Review Note: An Invariant Layer and Hard Safety Enforcement, Outside the Reach of Feedback Loops

## TL;DR

Every adaptive mechanism in this system — role commissioning, context governance, memory curation, model reassignment, system-level and meta-level feedback — is designed to change itself based on evidence. None of it currently sits on top of anything that is *structurally* exempt from that change. This note proposes that the project needs an explicit, human-authored, non-adaptive floor (goals, resource ceilings, inviolable constraints) plus a deterministic runtime enforcement mechanism beneath it — separate from ordinary capability/authority gating — because this is where alignment risk in this architecture actually concentrates: not in any single processor's output, but in the possibility that the system's own optimization process has no floor it cannot optimize away.

The enforcement half of that floor rests on one structural choice: **the gate binds effects, not roles.** The set has deliberately made its vocabulary of roles open-ended — feedback may commission actors that do not exist yet — so no enforcement mechanism can work by enumerating who is permitted to do what. Effects are a closed vocabulary; roles are not. The gate is therefore placed where the finite list is.

> **Motto:** Something must be true regardless of what the loops decide.

## Status

This is a forward note for later integration, not a proposed edit to any existing document. It should be read as flagging a structural gap discovered during conceptual review, not as a specification. The content here needs deliberate drafting with appropriate care, tooling, and — explicitly — grounding in existing AI safety and alignment literature before it becomes part of the documentation set proper. This is not a place to let a system (or an unreviewed model pass) design its own leash.

